            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-45409-python3-pip-whl",
              "name": "DpkgMatcherExactIndirectMatch",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-45409 medium vulnerability for python3-pip-whl package"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Internationalized Domain Names in Applications (IDNA) for Python provides support for Internationalized Domain Names in Applications (IDNA) and Unicode IDNA Compatibility Processing. In versions prior to 3.15, payloads such as `\"\\u0660\" * N` or `\"\\u30fb\" * N + \"\\u6f22\"` utilize the `valid_contexto` function prior to length rejection, and for high values of `N` will take a long time to process. This is the same issue as CVE-2024-3651, however the original remediation in 2024 was not a complete fix. A specially crafted argument to the `idna.encode()` function could consume significant resources. This may lead to a denial-of-service. Starting in version 3.14, the function rejects long inputs as soon as practicable prior to any further processing to minimize resource consumption. In version 3.15, this approach was extended to lesser used alternate functions (i.e. per-label conversions and codec support). A workaround is available. Domain names cannot exceed 253 characters in length. If this length limit is enforced prior to passing the domain to the `idna.encode()` function, it should no longer consume significant resources. This is triggered by arbitrarily large inputs that would not occur in normal usage, but may be passed to the library assuming there is no preliminary input validation by the higher-level application."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://ubuntu.com/security/CVE-2026-45409",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-45409\nSeverity: medium\nPackage: python3-pip-whl\nVersion: 24.0+dfsg-1ubuntu1.3\nFix Version: \nType: deb\nLocation: /var/lib/dpkg/info/python3-pip-whl.list\nData Namespace: ubuntu:distro:ubuntu:24.04\nLink: [CVE-2026-45409](https://ubuntu.com/security/CVE-2026-45409)",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-45409**\n| Severity | Package | Version | Fix Version | Type | Location | Data Namespace | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| medium  | python3-pip-whl  | 24.0+dfsg-1ubuntu1.3  |   | deb  | /var/lib/dpkg/info/python3-pip-whl.list  | ubuntu:distro:ubuntu:24.04  | [CVE-2026-45409](https://ubuntu.com/security/CVE-2026-45409)  |\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "purls": [
                  "pkg:deb/ubuntu/python3-pip-whl@24.0%2Bdfsg-1ubuntu1.3?arch=all\u0026distro=ubuntu-24.04\u0026upstream=python-pip"
                ],
                "security-severity": "6.9"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-95692-libssl3t64",
              "name": "DpkgMatcherExactIndirectMatch",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-95692 medium vulnerability for libssl3t64 package"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Issue summary: Setting an AES-GCM-SIV IV shorter than the expected nonce\nlength via EVP_CipherInit_ex() may trigger a heap out-of-bounds read.\nSupplying a longer IV is silently truncated instead of rejected.\n\nImpact summary: An application passing a caller-supplied IV of the wrong\nlength may crash or read beyond its buffer. Because trailing IV bytes are\nignored, an attacker who knows one message and tag may forge messages under a\ndifferent IV.\n\nCWE: CWE-125: Out-of-bounds Read\n\nDescription: AES-GCM-SIV copies the caller-supplied IV or nonce into its\ninternal state using a copy whose length is taken from the expected nonce size\nrather than from the length the caller actually provided. When the caller\npasses a shorter IV, the copy reads past the end of the caller's buffer;\nAddressSanitizer confirms a heap out-of-bounds read reached through\nEVP_CipherInit_ex() into ossl_aes_gcm_siv_init(). When the caller passes a\nlonger IV, the implementation silently discards the trailing bytes instead of\nrejecting the length.\n\nThe truncation breaks the binding between the IV and the computed\nauthentication tag. An attacker who has observed a message and its tag under\none IV can produce a message and tag that verify under a different IV whose\nfirst bytes match, since the surplus bytes never influence the computation.\nThe message itself is unchanged, so this is a failure of IV commitment rather\nthan a classic existential forgery.\n\nOnly applications that call EVP_CipherInit_ex() (or the equivalent provider\ncipher initialization) with an IV length other than the standard 12 bytes for\nAES-GCM-SIV are affected. The severity is limited because the out-of-bounds\nread is small and attacker-controlled only in length, and the forgery requires\nan unusual IV length choice by the application.\n\nFIPS impact: no\nThe FIPS modules are not affected by this issue, as AES-GCM-SIV is not an\napproved algorithm."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://ubuntu.com/security/CVE-2026-95692",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-95692\nSeverity: medium\nPackage: libssl3t64\nVersion: 3.0.13-0ubuntu3.16\nFix Version: \nType: deb\nLocation: /var/lib/dpkg/info/libssl3t64:amd64.md5sums\nData Namespace: ubuntu:distro:ubuntu:24.04\nLink: [CVE-2026-95692](https://ubuntu.com/security/CVE-2026-95692)",
